  "lesson": "## Your first song, and why it sounds right\n\n\n  Beethoven's melody uses only five notes (C D E F G) and moves almost entirely by step, which is why it is easy to sing and easy to play: your fingers 1 to 5 sit on C to G and never move.\n\n\n  The left hand alternates between C (the I chord) and G (the V chord). Notice bar 4 ends on D, a note inside the G chord, so it feels unfinished. Bar 8 ends on C, inside the C chord, so it feels done. That is a \"half cadence\" then a \"full cadence\": the question and the answer.\n\n\n  Bars 4 and 8 have a dotted rhythm (long-short). Count \"1-and-2-and\": the long note takes \"1 and 2\", the short one lands on \"and\".\n",
